to perform a similar violation

00:56:32.660 --> 00:56:36.390
but two figures who then remain passive

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:37.810
and therefore innocent.

00:56:37.810 --> 00:56:39.950
So in other words, it's as if he's saying

00:56:39.950 --> 00:56:41.890
he wants to exercise power

00:56:41.890 --> 00:56:45.810
but at the same time,
he claims to be innocent

00:56:45.810 --> 00:56:48.530
of the consequences of that exercise.

00:56:48.530 --> 00:56:50.400
So that's one way to read it.

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:51.897
The second way to read it is,

00:56:51.897 --> 00:56:54.780
and again, I don't get as deeply into this

00:56:54.780 --> 00:56:56.040
as I probably should

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:59.910
which is that you assume
he's been traumatized.

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:01.310
And the question of trauma of course

00:57:01.310 --> 00:57:03.440
is the trauma never resolved, right?

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:05.430
Trauma thrives in repetition

00:57:05.430 --> 00:57:07.790
because of its failure to fully represent

00:57:07.790 --> 00:57:09.180
what's actually happened.

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:12.010
So in the case of Duterte,
he likes to tell the story

00:57:12.010 --> 00:57:14.310
of being traumatized by the priest

00:57:14.310 --> 00:57:17.100
precisely because he can't
quite get over it, right?

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:18.650
It's something that goes on and on again,

00:57:18.650 --> 00:57:20.700
in that sense it probably resonates

00:57:20.700 --> 00:57:24.260
with the traumatic experiences
of his audiences too.

00:57:24.260 --> 00:57:26.590
That's what they share,
what they have in common

00:57:26.590 --> 00:57:29.070
is their inability to transcend our trauma

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:31.330
and therefore to be
condemned, to repeat it.

00:57:31.330 --> 00:57:35.050
But the payoff is the
fact that it's repeated

00:57:35.050 --> 00:57:38.660
but at the expense of
certain figures of power,

00:57:38.660 --> 00:57:40.710
in this case the priest or whoever it is,

00:57:41.680 --> 00:57:43.760
that's traumatized them.

00:57:43.760 --> 00:57:46.460
And there's a sort of
interesting connection

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:48.790
with his obsession with drug killings.

00:57:48.790 --> 00:57:52.620
I mean, you know, when, when
you hear him talk about addicts

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:55.190
and killing, I mean, he
basically it's the same thing.

00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:56.440
I want to kill as many of you

00:57:56.440 --> 00:57:58.610
motherfuckers as possible, right?

00:57:58.610 --> 00:58:01.310
I'm not gonna be happy until
everyone's dead, right?

00:58:01.310 --> 00:58:04.300
I want to kill, kill,
kill, kill, kill, right?

00:58:04.300 --> 00:58:07.110
So it's this, again, this repetitiveness

00:58:07.110 --> 00:58:11.117
this inability to be able
to sort of find closure

00:58:11.117 --> 00:58:13.950
and to return again and again

00:58:13.950 --> 00:58:18.650
to the situation of wanting
to violate without end.

00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:22.350
At the same time to maintain
that he is saving the country

00:58:22.350 --> 00:58:24.210
by killing addicts.

00:58:24.210 --> 00:58:27.540
And in that sense, sort of
maintain a certain innocence

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:30.690
even a certain nobility
in what he's doing.

00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:35.690
So it's again, just dialectic
of disclosure and domination

00:58:38.216 --> 00:58:40.680
that he's thrown back into

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:44.170
as he tries to repeat the same
thing, the same discourse,

00:58:44.170 --> 00:58:46.390
the same stories again and again.

00:58:46.390 --> 00:58:49.400
And this is what substitutes,
in case of Duterte,

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:51.680
this is what substitutes for policy.

00:58:51.680 --> 00:58:53.080
He doesn't have any policies,

00:58:53.080 --> 00:58:58.080
his only policy is to act
out as it were his trauma.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:59:01.690
If you want to, I mean that's
a little bit simplistic,

00:59:01.690 --> 00:59:04.050
but I mean, I think if you wanted to do

00:59:04.050 --> 00:59:08.747
like a psychopathological
analysis of what moves this guy,

00:59:08.747 --> 00:59:11.343
I mean, I think that's part of it.

00:59:11.343 --> 00:59:12.629
- Thank you.

00:59:12.629 --> 00:59:13.730
And then if we could also ask

00:59:13.730 --> 00:59:16.020
like a brief follow-up question.

00:59:16.020 --> 00:59:19.300
I was wondering if you'd
noticed any patterns

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:23.090
in his language use for
parts of his story setups,

00:59:23.090 --> 00:59:26.320
because you mentioned he
speaks in Tagalog as in English

00:59:26.320 --> 00:59:29.500
and are there, like does he
reserve a certain language

00:59:29.500 --> 00:59:32.680
for like certain punchlines
or like for certain setups

00:59:32.680 --> 00:59:35.590
or like building towards the climax?

00:59:35.590 --> 00:59:37.673
- Yeah, he likes to
speak in, Bisaya, Bisdak.

00:59:38.903 --> 00:59:40.303
Bisdak is his first language

00:59:41.240 --> 00:59:43.910
and some of his most vulgar

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:48.469
and more colorful anecdotes
are always in Bisdak.

00:59:48.469 --> 00:59:51.120
But Tagalog is his second,

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:54.250
actually English second
language Tagalog is his third.

00:59:54.250 --> 00:59:57.220
and Tigrish is probably his fourth, right?

00:59:57.220 --> 00:59:58.363
And so when he speaks,

00:59:59.430 --> 01:00:02.637
he sends two to navigate
through those different

01:00:02.637 --> 01:00:04.793
kinds of languages.

01:00:05.890 --> 01:00:07.348
And it's the, like

01:00:07.348 --> 01:00:11.680
the vaginas shooting the
communist rebels in the vagina,

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:14.800
which is so horrific you
can't even contemplate it,

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:17.100
but he likes to tell that in this Bisdak.

01:00:17.100 --> 01:00:18.353
So, yeah.

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:21.290
- Thank you so much.

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:23.921
- Thank you.

